    "closedAt": "2025-01-19T18:36:35Z",
    "comment_count": 1,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author": {
                "avatarUrl": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/24854248?u=d09fd974c6720eba3039db6beb2f1c1efecdb51d&v=4",
                "login": "echasnovski",
                "resourcePath": "/echasnovski",
                "url": "https://github.com/echasnovski"
            },
            "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Yes, because the whole 'mini.nvim' mostly relies on Vimscript `input()` (i.e. `vim.fn.input()`). This is because it is synchronous and returns the results compared to asynchronous `vim.ui.input()` which runs a callback. The latter usually requires a more complex module structure compared to the former. So to have a coherent experience, 'mini.visits' also uses `vim.fn.input()`.\r\n\r\nIf/when there is `vim.ui.input` implementation in 'mini.nvim' (which will probably be able to return the input synchronously while being non-blocking) or interface of `vim.ui.input` changes to allow something like `sync` options flag, 'mini.nvim' will probably move to `vim.ui.input` all at once.",
            "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">Yes, because the whole 'mini.nvim' mostly relies on Vimscript <code class=\"notranslate\">input()</code> (i.e. <code class=\"notranslate\">vim.fn.input()</code>). This is because it is synchronous and returns the results compared to asynchronous <code class=\"notranslate\">vim.ui.input()</code> which runs a callback. The latter usually requires a more complex module structure compared to the former. So to have a coherent experience, 'mini.visits' also uses <code class=\"notranslate\">vim.fn.input()</code>.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">If/when there is <code class=\"notranslate\">vim.ui.input</code> implementation in 'mini.nvim' (which will probably be able to return the input synchronously while being non-blocking) or interface of <code class=\"notranslate\">vim.ui.input</code> changes to allow something like <code class=\"notranslate\">sync</code> options flag, 'mini.nvim' will probably move to <code class=\"notranslate\">vim.ui.input</code> all at once.</p>",
            "bodyText": "Yes, because the whole 'mini.nvim' mostly relies on Vimscript input() (i.e. vim.fn.input()). This is because it is synchronous and returns the results compared to asynchronous vim.ui.input() which runs a callback. The latter usually requires a more complex module structure compared to the former. So to have a coherent experience, 'mini.visits' also uses vim.fn.input().\nIf/when there is vim.ui.input implementation in 'mini.nvim' (which will probably be able to return the input synchronously while being non-blocking) or interface of vim.ui.input changes to allow something like sync options flag, 'mini.nvim' will probably move to vim.ui.input all at once.",
